.\" Man page for Vuurmuur 0.8.2. Kept in sync with the option handling in
.\" vuurmuur_script/vuurmuur_script.c, the variable table in
.\" vuurmuur_script/backendcheck.h and the exit codes in vuurmuur_script.h.
.TH "VUURMUUR_SCRIPT" "8" "February 2025" "Vuurmuur 0.8.2" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
vuurmuur_script \- scriptable commandline tool for administration of the Vuurmuur IPTables frontend.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B vuurmuur_script \fB[\-ACDLMOPRSVdghinorsvz]\fR \fB[\-c\fR \fIconfig-file\fR\fB] [\-d\fR \fIlevel\fR\fB] \fB[\-S\fR \fIvalue\fR\fB] [\-V\fR \fIname\fR\fB] \fB[\-\-block\fR \fIname\fR\fB]\fR
.br
.RI "\ \fB[\-\-unblock\fR \fIname\fR\fB]\fR\fB [\-\-list-blocked]\fR\fB [\-\-list-devices]\fR\fB [\-\-list-paths]\fR\fB [\-\-reload]\fR"
.br
.RI "\ \fB[\-\-apply]\fR\fB [\-\-no-apply]\fR\fB [\-\-rule-numbers]\fR\fB [\-\-verbose]\fR\fB [\-\-version]\fR"
.br
.RI "\ \fB[\-c\fR \fIconfig-file\fR\fB]\fR\fB [\-d]\fR"
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBvuurmuur_script\fR creates, edits, lists and deletes the Vuurmuur objects
(zones, networks, hosts, groups, services, interfaces and rule sets) without
the ncurses interface, so that configuration can be scripted.
.PP
Every invocation must run as root: the check happens before the configuration
is read, so it applies to \fB\-\-help\fR and \fB\-\-version\fR as well.
.PP
An object is named by exactly one of the object options below. Names are
qualified by their parent, from the top down: a zone is
.IR zone ,
a network is
.IR network.zone ,
a host or group is
.IR host.network.zone .
The name
.B firewall
is reserved for the machine the daemon runs on: rules may name it as a source
or destination, and
.B \-\-rename
refuses a new name starting with it. Nothing stops an object from being
created under such a name, so keep them clear of it.
.PP
Changes are written to the backend immediately; they are not installed in the
kernel until something tells the daemons to reload, see \fB\-\-apply\fR and
\fB\-\-reload\fR below.
.SH "COMMANDS"
.TP
\fB\-C\fR, \fB\-\-create\fR
Create the named object, empty.
.TP
\fB\-D\fR, \fB\-\-delete\fR
Delete the named object.
.TP
\fB\-R\fR, \fB\-\-rename\fR
Rename the named object to the value of \fB\-S\fR.
.TP
\fB\-M\fR, \fB\-\-modify\fR
Set the variable named by \fB\-V\fR on the named object to the value of
\fB\-S\fR.
.TP
\fB\-L\fR, \fB\-\-list\fR
List objects of the given type. Without an object option, or with
.IR any ,
it lists everything of that type.
.TP
\fB\-P\fR, \fB\-\-print\fR
Print the variables of one object.
.TP
.BI \-\-block " name"
Add \fIname\fR to the block list. The argument is used literally: the tool
appends \fBblock\fR \fIname\fR to the block list rule set and does not check
that it resolves. Host and group names and addresses work; a network or zone
name is accepted here and discarded with a warning when the daemon loads the
list.
.TP
.BI \-\-unblock " name"
Remove the block list entry whose text matches \fIname\fR exactly. A host
blocked by name is therefore not removed by giving its address, or the other
way around.
.TP
.B \-\-list-blocked
List the entries currently in the block list.
.TP
.B \-\-list-devices
List the network devices of the host as \fIname ip netmask network broadcast\fR.
This one is recognised only as the first argument, and it is not
listed by \fB\-\-help\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-list-paths
Print the directories the installation uses: \fBSYSCONFDIR\fR,
\fBVUURMUURCONFDIR\fR, \fBCONFIGFILE\fR, \fBPLUGINDIR\fR and \fBDATADIR\fR.
\fBPLUGINDIR\fR is a leftover from before the backends were linked in, and
prints as its own name.
.TP
.B \-\-reload
Tell the daemons to reload their configuration. This overrides any
\fB\-C\fR, \fB\-D\fR, \fB\-R\fR, \fB\-M\fR, \fB\-P\fR or \fB\-L\fR command on the
same command line, and it implies \fB\-\-apply\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-apply
After the change, tell the daemons to load the new configuration, and wait for
both of them to answer. This is a shared memory handshake, not a signal, and it
gives up after 60 seconds. It is the default when \fB\-\-block\fR,
\fB\-\-unblock\fR or \fB\-\-reload\fR is used.
.TP
.B \-\-no-apply
Do not tell the daemons anything, even if \fB\-\-apply\fR was also given on
the command line.
.SH "OBJECT TYPES"
.TP
\fB\-z\fR, \fB\-\-zone\fR \fIname\fR
zone.
.TP
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-network\fR \fIname\fR
network.
.TP
\fB\-o\fR, \fB\-\-host\fR \fIname\fR
host.
.TP
\fB\-g\fR, \fB\-\-group\fR \fIname\fR
group.
.TP
\fB\-s\fR, \fB\-\-service\fR \fIname\fR
service.
.TP
\fB\-i\fR, \fB\-\-interface\fR \fIname\fR
interface.
.TP
\fB\-r\fR, \fB\-\-rule\fR \fIname\fR
rule set. Only the two names \fBrules\fR and \fBblocklist\fR are valid.
.SH "VARIABLES"
The name given to \fB\-V\fR is matched case sensitively. The value given to
\fB\-S\fR is not: the accepted spellings (\fBYes\fR/\fBNo\fR, \fBdynamic\fR,
\fBkbit\fR/\fBmbit\fR) are matched case insensitively, so \fB\-\-set yes\fR and
\fB\-\-set DYNAMIC\fR both take effect, while a value matching none of them is
rejected with exit status 1: the validators return \fIVRS_ERR_COMMANDLINE\fR,
\fBbackend_check()\fR in \fIvuurmuur_script/backendcheck.c\fR hands that value
straight back, and \fBscript_modify()\fR propagates it unchanged, so a bad value
and a bad command line are not separable by status alone. Which variables exist
depends on the
object type; the ones marked \fImultiple\fR hold a list and accept \fB\-A\fR.
.TP
.B zone
\fBACTIVE\fR, \fBCOMMENT\fR
.TP
.B network
\fBACTIVE\fR, \fBNETWORK\fR, \fBNETMASK\fR, \fBINTERFACE\fR (multiple),
\fBRULE\fR (multiple), \fBCOMMENT\fR
.TP
.B host
\fBACTIVE\fR, \fBIPADDRESS\fR, \fBMAC\fR, \fBCOMMENT\fR
.TP
.B group
\fBACTIVE\fR, \fBMEMBER\fR (multiple), \fBCOMMENT\fR
.TP
.B service
\fBACTIVE\fR, \fBTCP\fR, \fBUDP\fR, \fBICMP\fR, \fBGRE\fR, \fBESP\fR, \fBAH\fR,
\fBPROTO_41\fR (all multiple), \fBBROADCAST\fR, \fBHELPER\fR, \fBCOMMENT\fR
.TP
.B interface
\fBACTIVE\fR, \fBDEVICE\fR, \fBIPADDRESS\fR, \fBVIRTUAL\fR, \fBRULE\fR
(multiple), \fBSHAPE\fR, \fBTCPMSS\fR, \fBBW_IN\fR, \fBBW_OUT\fR,
\fBBW_IN_UNIT\fR, \fBBW_OUT_UNIT\fR, \fBCOMMENT\fR
.TP
.B rule set
\fBRULE\fR (multiple)
.TP
\fB\-V\fR, \fB\-\-variable\fR \fIname\fR
the variable to modify or print.
.TP
\fB\-S\fR, \fB\-\-set\fR \fIvalue\fR
the value to set, or the new name when renaming. An empty value with
\fB\-O\fR clears the variable.
.TP
\fB\-A\fR, \fB\-\-append\fR
Add the value to a multiple-valued variable instead of replacing what it
holds.
.TP
\fB\-O\fR, \fB\-\-overwrite\fR
Replace the value of a multiple-valued variable. This is the default.
.SH "PRINT OPTIONS"
.TP
.B \-\-rule-numbers
With \fB\-P \-V RULE\fR, prefix each rule with its number, which is what
\fB\-\-unblock\fR and a rule delete need.
.SH "OTHER OPTIONS"
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Give a summary of the command line options and exit with status 0.
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Verbose mode: messages go to stdout as well as to the log files.
.TP
\fB\-c\fR \fIfile\fR, \fB\-\-configfile\fR \fIfile\fR
Use \fIfile\fR instead of the default configuration file.
.TP
\fB\-d\fR \fIlevel\fR
Enable debugging, 1 low, 3 high. The \fB\-\-debug\fR spelling is declared as
taking no argument while the handler reads one, so it is unusable:
\fB\-\-debug 3\fR dereferences a null pointer and dies with \fBSIGSEGV\fR (shell
status 139) - it would otherwise leave the \fI3\fR to be parsed as a non-option
argument - and \fB\-\-debug=3\fR takes the unrecognised-option path and prints
the help. Use
the short form \fB\-d\fR \fI3\fR.
.TP
\fB\-V\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Print the version of vuurmuur_script and libvuurmuur in use and exit.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP 0.4i
0
success
.TP
1
bad command line: unusable or missing arguments, an object name of \fIany\fR
for a command that needs a real object, a rename to a name starting with
\fBfirewall\fR, a ruleset name other than \fBrules\fR or \fBblocklist\fR, or
not running as root. The same status 1 also means the configuration file or
the backends could not be opened, so a broken \fIconfig.conf\fR and a typo are
not distinguishable by exit status alone.
.TP
2
the command itself failed
.TP
3
the object was not found
.TP
4
the object already exists
.TP
5
out of memory
.TP
6
the backend holds data this tool cannot make sense of
.TP
254
internal error
.PP
An option the parser does not recognise at all prints the help and exits 0, so
a script that typos a flag will look like it succeeded. Check for it explicitly
if it matters. A letter that is in the option string but has no handler, such
as \fB\-B\fR, takes the other path: an error message and exit status 1.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B Give a list of all groups:
\fBvuurmuur_script\fR \-\-list \-\-group any
.TP
.B Give a list of all groups in network localnet.lan:
\fBvuurmuur_script\fR \-\-list \-\-group localnet.lan
.TP
.B Print the content of host server.localnet.lan:
\fBvuurmuur_script\fR \-\-print \-\-host server.localnet.lan
.TP
.B Create an empty host:
\fBvuurmuur_script\fR \-\-create \-\-host pc1.localnet.lan
.TP
.B Set the host to active:
\fBvuurmuur_script\fR \-\-modify \-\-host pc1.localnet.lan \-\-variable ACTIVE \-\-set Yes
.TP
.B Set the ip address of the new host:
\fBvuurmuur_script\fR \-\-modify \-\-host pc1.localnet.lan \-\-variable IPADDRESS \-\-set 192.168.1.15
.TP
.B Append a rule to the rules list:
\fBvuurmuur_script\fR \-M \-r rules \-V RULE \-S "accept service ftp from pc1.localnet.lan to firewall" \-A
.TP
.B Add the address 1.2.3.4 to the block list and install it:
\fBvuurmuur_script\fR \-\-block 1.2.3.4
.TP
.B Remove that address from the block list:
\fBvuurmuur_script\fR \-\-unblock 1.2.3.4
.TP
.B Change a service without touching the running firewall:
\fBvuurmuur_script\fR \-M \-s ftp \-V TCP \-S "21" \-\-no-apply
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/vuurmuur/config.conf
Configuration file, unless another one was given with \fB\-c\fR. The
\fIetcdir\fR part is fixed at build time unless \fBVUURMUUR_SYSCONFDIR\fR is
set; see ENVIRONMENT.
.TP
.I /etc/vuurmuur/plugins/textdir.conf
Where the \fBtextdir\fR backend is told to keep its tree (\fBLOCATION\fR).
Under it the objects live as \fIzones/<zone>/zone.config\fR,
\fIzones/<zone>/networks/<net>/network.config\fR,
\fI.../hosts/<host>.host\fR, \fI.../groups/<group>.group\fR,
\fIservices/<service>\fR, \fIinterfaces/<interface>.conf\fR and
\fIrules/rules.conf\fR.
Each is a set of \fINAME="value"\fR lines; a leading \fB;\fR in a rule value
marks that rule inactive.
.TP
.I <logdir>/vuurmuur.log\fR, \fI<logdir>/error.log
Where the tool's messages also go; \fI<logdir>\fR is the \fBLOGDIR\fR setting
of the configuration file, \fI/var/log/vuurmuur\fR unless that setting says
otherwise.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B VUURMUUR_SYSCONFDIR
Overrides the compiled-in configuration directory: the config file is then
.IR $VUURMUUR_SYSCONFDIR /vuurmuur/config.conf
and the textdir backend's object tree moves with it, because the backend finds
it through the configuration. A warning is logged whenever the variable is in
use. Setting it makes the tool read a different tree from the one the daemons
use, which is the point when pointing at a scratch install and the hazard when
it is forgotten.
.SH HISTORY
The \fB\-B\fR letter is in the option string but has no handler: it is reported
as an unknown option and the tool exits 1. The long option \fB\-\-debug\fR is
declared without an argument while its handler reads one, so it cannot be used;
both are candidates for removal. The debug handler also prints
\fIvuurmuur: debugging enabled.\fR from inside this program, in \fBmain()\fR of
\fIvuurmuur_script/vuurmuur_script.c\fR, which matters when the question
is which of the two binaries is the one misbehaving.
